<p>Guests trust other guests. Before they book, most people scan your star rating, how many reviews you have and how recent they are. Google weighs the same signals when it decides which restaurants to show on the map. The good news: you can <strong>get more Google reviews</strong> consistently with a simple system, without bribes, fake accounts or anything that risks your listing.</p>
<h2>First, the rules you must not break</h2>
<p>Google's <a href="https://support.google.com/contributionpolicy/answer/7400114">review policies</a> prohibit a few common shortcuts. Avoid all of them:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No incentives.</strong> Do not offer discounts, free desserts or entries into a draw in exchange for a review.</li>
<li><strong>No review gating.</strong> Do not ask only happy guests, or send unhappy ones somewhere else first. Ask everyone the same way.</li>
<li><strong>No reviews from staff, family or bought accounts.</strong> Google detects patterns, and removed reviews or a warning on your listing cost far more than they gain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Everything below stays inside these rules.</p>
<h2>Ask at the moment of delight</h2>
<p>The best time to ask is when the guest is happiest: right after they compliment a dish, at the end of a celebration, or when they thank the server on the way out. Train your team to recognize those moments and use one natural line:</p>
<blockquote>"I'm so glad you enjoyed it. It would mean a lot to us if you shared that on Google. It really helps a small restaurant like ours."</blockquote>
<p>Personal, specific and short. Servers who mention their own name often see guests mention them in the review, which is great for morale too.</p>
<h2>Make it a one-tap action</h2>
<p>Every extra step loses reviews. Get your direct review link from your Google Business Profile ("Ask for reviews") and put it everywhere:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>QR code</strong> on table tents, the check presenter and the receipt</li>
<li>Your reservation and online-ordering confirmation emails</li>
<li>A short follow-up text or email the day after a reservation</li>
<li>Your website footer and your link-in-bio</li>
</ul>
<h3>The follow-up message that works</h3>
<p>If your reservation or POS system can send a message after the visit, keep it human: thank them for coming, mention you hope to see them again, and include the review link. Send it within 24 hours, while the meal is still fresh. Our <a href="/services/email-sms">email and SMS team</a> sets these flows up so they run on their own.</p>
<h2>Reply to every review</h2>
<p>Replies show future guests that someone is listening, and they keep reviewers engaged. Aim to respond within two days.</p>
<h3>Replying to positive reviews</h3>
<p>Thank them by name, mention something specific they said ("so happy the birria tacos hit the spot"), and invite them back. Vary your wording; copy-pasted replies look careless.</p>
<h3>Replying to negative reviews</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stay calm and never argue in public. Other guests are reading your reply more than the reviewer is.</li>
<li>Acknowledge the experience and apologize for how it felt, without making excuses.</li>
<li>Take it offline: give a name and a direct way to reach a manager.</li>
<li>Fix the cause. Repeated complaints about wait times or a dish are free operational feedback.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a review breaks Google's policies (spam, hate speech, a review about the wrong business), report it from your profile. Do not report reviews just because they are negative.</p>
<h2>Track it weekly</h2>
<p>Pick three numbers and look at them every Monday: new reviews this week, average rating of those new reviews, and replies still pending. Set a realistic weekly goal per location and celebrate the team when you hit it. Momentum matters more than a one-time push.</p>
<h2>Turn reviews into marketing</h2>
<p>Great reviews are content. Share standout quotes on Instagram stories, feature them on your website and in your newsletter, and use the words guests use to describe you in your ads. Pair this with a strong <a href="/insights/google-business-profile-for-restaurants">Google Business Profile</a> and you will see the effect on calls and direction requests.</p>
